      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kenel takes its name from Father Martin Joseph Kenel, a Swiss-born Benedictine priest who served as a school superintendent in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenel%2C_South_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office operated in Kenel from 1914 until 1963, formalizing the community's identity during early twentieth-century settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenel%2C_South_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The region near Kenel includes the site of Fort Manuel Lisa, a fur trading post built in 1812 to facilitate river commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kenel"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Kenel is situated within the unglaciated section of the Missouri Plateau, characterized by gently rolling prairies and scattered buttes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kenel"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Kline Butte Storage Reservoir, a three point six million dollar project, is located southwest of Kenel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenel%2C_South_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Local grasslands around Kenel support diverse wildlife, including black-tailed prairie dogs and pronghorn antelope.",
